    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7e647793_e0c3c9eb",
        "filename": "system/btif/src/btif_sock_rfc.cc",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 968,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-04T05:40:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`handle_discovery_comp` is only called when we got a `BTA_JV_DISCOVERY_COMP_EVT` from Jv. We don\u0027t need (or actually shouldn\u0027t) call `BTA_JvCancelDiscovery` in that.\n\n`BTA_JV_DISCOVERY_COMP_EVT` could come from 3 places:\n* 1 place in `bta_jv_start_discovery_cback`: We don\u0027t need to call cancel as this cback is exactly called when the discovery is done\n* 2 places in `bta_jv_start_discovery`: These are the failure cases. If we failed on the `sdp_active` check, then we SHOULD NOT cancel the discovery because that\u0027s scheduled by somebody else and should be cleaned up when they is closed. If we failed on the `SDP_ServiceSearchAttributeRequest2` call, then Jv should have cleaned up the state and we don\u0027t need to cancel either\n\nSo this is why I said we just shouldn\u0027t call cancel in handle_discovery_comp.\n\nThe conclusion keeps in the perspective of the API design. When the low layer (Jv) has told us a request has \"completed\", I don\u0027t see any reason to explicitly \"cancel\" the request.",
      "parentUuid": "39f64df6_97985509",
